# These are templates, not build inputs. They are meant to be copied
# when starting a new fastfile project and then edited there. Still,
# let's make all the files targets so that they make it into the
# distribution and a source distribution remains sufficient for creating
# a project from scratch.
#
# There is no corresponding install=false here. file{} is already not
# installed by default, which gives us exactly the semantics we want.
#
# Now, there is a small wrinkle with empty directories. Git does not
# track them, so normally we could put a .gitkeep into each one. That
# does not quite work here: build2 wildcard matching ignores a name
# starting with '.' if the pattern itself does not start with one. In
# other words, file{**} would not see such a placeholder and the
# directory would still disappear from the distribution.
#
# We could invent another placeholder convention, but every template
# directory needs an explanation anyway. So put a README.md into each
# directory and let that be the file which keeps it around. This has the
# nice property that after copying the template there is something in
# each directory explaining what is expected to go there.
#
./: file{** -buildfile}

# And let's check this convention here. Counting directories against
# README.md files is sufficient since each README lives directly in the
# directory it documents. So adding a directory without its README, or
# removing one later, makes the build fail as soon as this buildfile is
# loaded instead of silently dropping that part of the template from the
# distribution.
#
template_dirs    = $path_search('fastfile/**/', $src_base)
template_readmes = $path_search('fastfile/**/README.md', $src_base)

assert ($size($template_dirs) == $size($template_readmes)) "every directory under templates/fastfile/ must carry a README.md; see templates/fastfile.dirs for the directory manifest"
